Working on dirk
===============

CONTRIBUTING.md is the social half: what a good change looks like, and what the
code is trying to be. This is the mechanical half — the loop, from a fresh
clone to a merged pull request, and how a release is cut.


One command, once
-----------------

    $ git clone https://github.com/oddurs/dirk && cd dirk
    $ make setup

`make setup` configures this checkout, and only this checkout:

  * `git work`, the alias that drives everything below.
  * `core.hooksPath = .githooks`, so the hooks in the repository are the hooks
    that run.
  * rerere, so a conflict solved once is not solved again on the next rebase.
  * `merge.conflictstyle = zdiff3`, which shows the common ancestor. The
    difference between reading a conflict and guessing at one.
  * autosetupremote, autostash, autosquash, prune on fetch, histogram diffs,
    branches sorted by date rather than alphabet.

It touches nothing global. Nothing here is a preference imposed on you; it is
what the workflow below assumes.


The loop
--------

Every branch gets a worktree of its own. Two pieces of work can then be in
flight at once without sharing a build directory — which matters here, because
much of this is written by coding agents, and an agent that has to `git switch`
is an agent that corrupts whatever else is running.

    $ git work start 44
    claimed 0044 — Create and manage worktrees from the nav
    /Users/you/Code/.dirk-worktrees/create-and-manage-worktrees

`start` takes a backlog id or a branch name. Given an id it reads the title,
claims the item so nobody duplicates the work, derives the branch name, and
branches from `origin/main` rather than from wherever you happened to be.

The link between the branch and the item is kept in `branch.<name>.cairn-item`,
which is what per-branch git config is for. Nothing has to be remembered and
nothing has to be passed twice.

    $ cd /Users/you/Code/.dirk-worktrees/create-and-manage-worktrees
    $ ... work ...
    $ git work sync           # rebase onto origin/main, when it has moved
    $ git work ship

`ship` closes the item and commits that, runs the gate, pushes, opens a pull
request titled from the item and bodied from its reasoning, and turns on
auto-merge.

The close is in the branch on purpose. `land` runs in the trunk checkout, after
the branch it belonged to is gone, so anything it wrote to `cairn/items` there
would be a loose edit with nowhere to go — the trunk takes changes only through
a pull request. Putting the close in the branch makes the commit that did the
work the commit that recorded it. When CI goes green GitHub
squashes it and deletes the branch. `git work ship --land` waits for that to
happen and then does the last step for you.

    $ git work land           # remove the worktree; ship closed the item
    $ git work drop           # change your mind: hand the item back instead
    $ git work list           # what is still in flight
    $ git work clean          # reap everything that has already merged

`land` is safe to forget. `clean`, run from the main checkout, finds every
worktree whose branch is now an ancestor of `origin/main`, closes its item, and
removes it.

Abandoning is not landing, and the difference matters: `drop` hands the item
back to the backlog rather than closing it, because nothing was finished.
`land` refuses a branch with no commits and no pull request for the same
reason — closing an item because nothing was done to it is how a backlog
quietly loses work.

Worktrees live in a sibling directory — `../.dirk-worktrees` by default,
`$DIRK_WORKTREES` if you want them elsewhere — so nothing nests inside a
checkout and there is no ignore rule to maintain.


The gate
--------

    $ make check

`cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `shellcheck`
over the scripts and hooks, and the full test suite. It is what CI runs, and `git work ship` runs it before it pushes,
so a red pull request should be a surprise.

    $ make shot

prints what dirk currently paints, as plain text. The fastest way to see
whether a change to the chrome did what you meant, and it pastes into an issue
far better than a screenshot of a terminal does.


Seeing it run
-------------

    $ make dev           # once, anywhere in the repository
    $ dirk-dev           # in any terminal: attach to the dev session

`make dev` builds, then watches `src`, `tests` and the manifest in this
checkout and in every worktree beside it, and on every change builds the tree
that changed and hands the running `dev` session over to the result with
`session handoff`. Every shell and agent in the session survives, because a
handoff is an `exec` and not a restart, and the attached terminal comes back
on its own as the new binary. A build that fails leaves the session on what it
had, and the log says which tree and commit it is running.

Every tree rather than this one because the loop puts each piece of work in a
worktree of its own: an agent editing one of them sees its change running
without anyone restarting the watcher there. When two trees change, the later
one wins.

The session runs from a copy of the binary under `~/.cache/dirk/dev`, not from
any `target/`, which is what lets one session be handed builds from several
trees: the path a server execs is its own, so it has to be the same path every
time. It is a separate session from your everyday `dirk`, with its own name
and socket, and the two binaries never meet.

Handing off is off by default, for the reason `[session] handoff` gives in the
manual; `make dev` needs it on in `~/.config/dirk/config.toml`:

    [session]
    handoff = true


Testing a program that draws a terminal
---------------------------------------

The naming policy in `src/name.rs` is pure and is unit tested directly.
Everything else is a terminal talking to a terminal, and the only honest way to
test that is to give it one: `tests/smoke.rs` spawns the real binary on a real
pseudo-terminal, reads what it paints, and drives it with real keystrokes.

One trap, because it has already caught someone: the harness drains the
pseudo-terminal on its own thread, always. A test that reads only while waiting
for output fills the buffer the moment it stops, dirk blocks in `write`, and the
next keystroke is never processed — which looks exactly like dirk ignoring your
input.


The website
-----------

`site/` is a workspace member, and `cargo run -p site -- build` is the whole of
it. It is a cargo target rather than an off-the-shelf generator because half of
what belongs on the site is generated from the program.

    $ make site          build into site/dist
    $ make site-serve    serve it, and rebuild when anything it reads changes
    $ make shots         regenerate the terminal renders from the real binary

The palette comes out of `src/theme.rs`, the roadmap out of `ROADMAP.md`, the
changelog out of `NEWS`, and the screens out of the real binary on a real
pseudo-terminal. Nothing on the site is a hand-copied duplicate of something in
the repository, and nothing new should be.

`site-serve` watches `src/theme.rs` as well as `site/`, so a colour changed in
the program shows up in the browser without restarting anything.

Two things about it are worth knowing before changing much. The site is served
under `/dirk/` and built under `/` locally, so every internal link goes through
the base path — one written straight into a template works in development and
404s in production. And the terminal renders under `site/shots` are committed
rather than built on demand: a site build should never need to allocate a
pseudo-terminal, and a change to the chrome should show up in a diff and be
looked at.

A push to main that touches any of that deploys the site. A pull request builds
it without deploying, so a change that breaks it fails in review.


The backlog
-----------

The roadmap and the issue tracker are the same thing, and both are files in the
repository, kept with cairn <https://github.com/oddurs/cairn>.

    $ cairn next          # what is ready to start
    $ cairn show 44       # the reasoning that produced it
    $ cairn claim 44      # take it
    $ cairn check         # must pass before the work is finished

There are no ad-hoc TODO, PLAN or NOTES files in this repository, and there
should not be: an item carries the same information, appears on the board, and
arrives in the generated roadmap. `ROADMAP.md` is generated — never edit it.

Two passes fill it from outside the day's work, and both are written down so
they can be run again rather than reinvented. `doc/parity-herdr.md` compares
dirk to herdr and says what is worth taking. `doc/audit.md` goes over the tree
looking for what is wrong with it rather than what is missing from it — worth a
pass after a run of feature work has landed.


Agents
------

`AGENTS.md` is the brief every coding agent reads. `.claude/agents/` holds
subagents for the jobs this repository actually has: the backlog, the pty smoke
tests, the chrome, NEWS, review, and the documentation. `.claude/commands/`
holds the loop above, so `/ship` is the same thing `git work ship` is.

The history says who decided something, not which program typed it.
`.githooks/commit-msg` strips tool attribution from every commit message —
`Co-authored-by` trailers naming a tool, generated-with footers — regardless of
which harness is running. Human co-authors are left alone.


Style
-----

`cargo fmt` decides formatting; do not argue with it in review. Comments say
why, not what — the code already says what. A comment recording a decision, a
trap, or something that was tried and rejected is worth keeping; one that
narrates the next line is not.

Commit subjects are lowercase, colon-prefixed by area, and say what changed
rather than what was done: `boards: a row that reports without being opened`.
The body says why, in prose, with a GNU-style file list at the end when the
change touches more than one place.


Cutting a release
-----------------

NEWS is the changelog. Release notes are cut from it rather than written twice,
which means writing it is the first step and not the last.

    $ $EDITOR NEWS                # a new "* Noteworthy changes in release X"
    $ make news                   # what the release notes will say
    $ git work release 0.5.0      # branch, bump, commit, pull request
    ... it lands ...
    $ git work tag 0.5.0          # tag main, which cuts the release

The tag is the trigger. `.github/workflows/release.yml` refuses to build if the
tag, `Cargo.toml` and NEWS disagree about the version — a release whose notes
belong to a different version is worse than no release — then builds five
targets, tars each with the README, COPYING, NEWS and the manual page, adds the
source tarball from `make dist`, writes SHA256SUMS, and publishes.

Nothing about a release happens outside the ordinary loop except the tag
itself.


